Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "player absence",
    "offensive reorganization",
    "initiation profile similarity",
    "replacement player gains",
    "play-type redistribution",
    "primary play types"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to define a player's pre-absence initiation profile (time window, play types included, similarity metric)?",
    "How to identify the absent player's primary play types (frequency threshold, stability)?",
    "How to measure the largest gainer: absolute increase in initiations, share, or efficiency in those play types?",
    "How to select the candidate teammate pool: closest profile, most minutes, most initiations, same-position minutes?",
    "What is the appropriate unit of analysis: absence spell, player, team?",
    "How to account for confounding by coaching decisions, game situations, and opponent adjustments?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-052",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "Hypothesis: Having a similar-profile teammate separates absorption from cascade when a player sits. Result: mean similarity ABSORBED +0.863 vs CASCADE +0.848; Spearman(sim, -gap) = -0.19 (wrong-signed). Embiid 23-24 cascades with Reed sim 0.94. Verdict: null (adequately powered null result). Failure evidence: Similarity does not separate absorption from cascade; almost always findable."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF19",
      "status_as_recorded": "supported",
      "finding": "Hypothesis: A mover's displacement signature is more similar across franchises than profile-matched destination decoys' signatures. Confirmed with Delta>0, permutation p=0.005, positive sign test. Not about player absence."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-PAP-016",
      "status_as_recorded": "supported",
      "finding": "Feasibility gate for portability study: enough movers with decoys, displacement signature not collinear with play-type profile (rho=0.007). Not about player absence."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-044",
      "status_as_recorded": "supported",
      "finding": "Oracle BEST player for a counter play type varies by opponent, not a single team identity. Not about player absence."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-052",
      "what_failed": "Using overall similarity of teammate initiation profile to predict whether the absent player's play types are absorbed or cascade to others",
      "why": "(B_powered_null) Similarity does not separate absorption from cascade; a similar-profile teammate is almost always findable, so the predictor lacks discriminative power. Label/classification error risk present."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "A prior exploratory study (23 absences) found that similarity of teammate profile did not discriminate between absorption and cascade outcomes, with a wrong-signed correlation and similar mean similarity across outcomes.",
    "The failure was deemed an adequately powered null result, indicating the effect is likely absent or too small to detect."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether a more specific metric (closest pre-absence initiation profile, most minutes/initiations/same-position minutes) could succeed if tested with a larger, more clearly defined sample.",
    "Whether the gain in the absent player's primary types (as opposed to the absorption/cascade classification) is predictable.",
    "The statistical power of the earlier study was not recorded, so the adequacy of the null result is uncertain, though the project judged it adequate."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The current research question is a refined version of the hypothesis tested in FAM:SF-CHT-052, substituting 'largest gainer in primary types' for 'absorption vs cascade', but still relying on profile similarity to predict redistribution.",
    "The earlier finding that a similar-profile teammate is almost always findable suggests that merely identifying the closest match is not a strong test; one would need to show that the closest match gains more than other teammates, not just that something is absorbed or cascaded.",
    "With only 11 cases (as recorded) out of 148 spells, the sample is extremely sparse for a between-teammate comparison; power will be critically low.",
    "There are many confounders: coaching decisions, defensive adjustments, and natural variance in play-type frequencies."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The recorded unit description 'n=148; 148 spells; 11 cases' is ambiguous: does 'cases' refer to distinct absent players, player-absence combinations with identifiable closest-profile teammates, or something else? This drastically affects power.",
    "No details on how primary types would be defined or how the gain metric is operationalized, making it hard to assess measurement reliability.",
    "The earlier study's result may reflect a different operationalization (absorption vs cascade as a binary outcome) and might not fully pre-register a test of the current graded hypothesis."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "The retrieved null result (FAM:SF-CHT-052) shows that a closely related hypothesis failed because profile similarity was always findable and did not predict redistribution. Without that evidence, one might have hoped that the closest-profile teammate naturally steps in; the historical failure strongly suggests otherwise and, combined with the very low case count (11), pushes the decision to do_not_proceed."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.15,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "Only 11 cases (as per recorded units) for comparing closest-profile teammate gain to other teammates; inadequate for powered inference."
      },
      {
        "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
        "text": "Defining 'primary types' and 'largest gainer' from noisy play-type records invites misclassification; prior related study flagged label/classification error risk."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Coach rotations, opponent adjustments, and game situation influence who actually gains, confounding the profile similarity effect."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "Many absence spells may lack a clear closest-profile teammate or sufficient on-court minutes to observe a redistribution."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "partly",
    "cited": [
      "FAM:SF-CHT-052"
    ],
    "summary": "The project already tested whether similarity of a teammate's profile predicts absorption vs cascade and found a null result (inconclusive, powered null). This suggests the core idea lacks support, though the precise current hypothesis (closest-profile teammate gains most primary types) was not explicitly tested."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "Recorded units: 148 spells but only 11 cases. If cases represent the units for comparison, power is critically low."
    },
    {
      "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
      "text": "Classification of primary play types and gain magnitude from play-by-play derived data may be noisy, especially for short absence windows."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Coaching strategy and game flow dominate offensive role redistribution, making profile similarity a weak predictor."
    },
    {
      "code": "MECHANICAL_FLOOR",
      "text": "The closest-profile teammate may already have a high initiation share, introducing a ceiling or floor effect that distorts gain measurement."
    },
    {
      "code": "BASE_RATE",
      "text": "Primary play types may be team-specific and may not be 'vacated' in a straightforward way; they could be abandoned rather than filled."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.03,
    "rejected": 0.45,
    "untestable": 0.30,
    "artifact": 0.07,
    "uncertain": 0.15
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The difference between the closest-profile teammate's increase (absolute or share) in the absent player's primary play types during the absence spell and the average increase of other eligible teammates, controlling for baseline usage.",
    "unit": "Absence spell, with at least 3 games absent and a clear identification of a single closest pre-absence initiation profile (Euclidean or cosine similarity over play-type share vectors in last 15 pre-absence games).",
    "sample": "Spells from NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 where the absent player had ≥50 initiations in the pre-absence window, primary types defined as types accounting for ≥50% of initiations, and at least one teammate with >0 similarity and >20 minutes played during absence.",
    "power": "Likely unattainable with 11 available cases; would require ≥80 spells with clear closest-profile candidates to detect a medium effect (d≈0.5) at 80% power. The current data cannot provide this.",
    "controls": "Fixed effects for team and season; control for teammate baseline share of those play types before absence; opponent defensive strength.",
    "falsification": "If profile similarity truly drives gain, a dose-response relationship should be seen: larger similarity gap between closest and second-closest should predict larger gain gap. Also, the effect should not disappear when restricting to same-position teammates only.",
    "multiplicity": "Bonferroni correction if multiple candidate metrics (minutes, initiations, same-position minutes) are tested.",
    "decision_rule": "One-tailed paired t-test (or Wilcoxon) comparing closest-profile teammate's gain vs. other teammates' average gain, with alpha=0.05 and pre-registered alpha spent across if multiple definitions tested. Reject null if effect is positive and 95% CI excludes zero.",
    "data_version": "Play-by-play derived initiation records from NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 seasons."
  }
}

